before:
Object.keys(record) returns enumerable String keys, excluding Symbols. Reflect.ownKeys(record) includes own String and Symbol keys, so Symbol-keyed data can still be discovered. The Symbol is not encryption or an access-control boundary.
```javascript
function taggedRecord(label) {
  const token = Symbol("note");
  const record = {title: "public", [token]: label};
  return {token, record};
}
const first = taggedRecord("hidden label");
const second = taggedRecord("hidden label");
console.log(first.token === second.token);
console.log(first.record[first.token]);
console.log(Object.keys(first.record).join(","));
console.log(Reflect.ownKeys(first.record).length);
```
Output: false, hidden label, title, 2 on separate lines.

after:
Here is the complete example.
```javascript
function taggedRecord(label) {
  const token = Symbol("note");
  const record = {title: "public", [token]: label};
  return {token, record};
}
const first = taggedRecord("hidden label");
const second = taggedRecord("hidden label");
console.log(first.token === second.token);
console.log(first.record[first.token]);
console.log(Object.keys(first.record).join(","));
console.log(Reflect.ownKeys(first.record).length);
```
Output:
```text
false
hidden label
title
2
```
The tokens are different. `Object.keys` lists only the text key title. `Reflect.ownKeys` finds both own keys, including the Symbol.

A Symbol key is not secret: code can still discover it.
